# CV project agent standards

These rules are mandatory for every proposal, edit, commit, and push in this repo.

## 1. AAA modular design (source quality)

Before creating, changing, proposing, or pushing anything, cross-examine it:

> Is this following modular AAA design patterns?

Treat source quality as first-class. Prefer:

- Clear separation of concerns (structure / presentation / behavior)
- Small, reusable modules over one-off dumps
- Predictable naming and file layout
- Extensible patterns (easy to add a portfolio item, section, or page without rewriting the base)
- No fragile copy-paste duplication when a shared module would do
- Production-minded CSS/HTML/JS: intentional, maintainable, not throwaway

If a change fails that AAA modular check, redesign it before shipping.

When "AAA modular" is ambiguous for a given change, ask the user before proceeding.

## 2. PC + mobile parity (PC primary)

- **Primary target:** desktop / PC layout and polish
- **Required:** iPad and mobile must show the **same high visual quality**, not a degraded fallback
- Design and implement for both from the start — never "fix later"
- Touch targets, type scale, spacing, nav, and interactive controls must feel intentional on iPad
- Avoid patterns that look fine on PC but break on mobile (hover-only UX, tiny hit areas, overflow, fixed desktop assumptions)

## 3. Ask questions aggressively

- Whenever even **slightly** unsure about intent, content, design, copy, structure, or scope — **ask**
- Do not limit how many questions you ask; more clarity is better than guessing
- Prefer short, concrete questions (options when helpful)
- Do not invent portfolio entries, bio facts, job history, or visual direction when uncertain
- Asking is required behavior, not optional politeness

## 4. Visual QA before every push (hard gate)

Every time you write or change UI, spend extra effort on testing. **Do not push until visual quality is verified on both PC and mobile viewports.**

Minimum bar:

- Main test: **visual quality parity** (PC and mobile/iPad-width)
- Explicitly check known traps, e.g. a styled button on PC becoming a default blue underlined link on iPad/Safari
- Verify nav, buttons, links, typography, spacing, expand/collapse, and layout at desktop width **and** iPad/mobile widths
- Use available browser / screenshot / responsive tooling in the environment to compare
- If you cannot verify a surface, say so and ask before pushing

**Push rule:** no push of UI changes without completed visual QA for PC + mobile parity.
